    <sec id="s1">
      <title>1. Introduction</title>
      <p>Spillover is frequent in the dry season<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="R1">1</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="R2">2</xref></sup>. Earlier surveys agree [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="R1">1</xref>–<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="R3">3</xref>], as does a field study (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="R2">Smith et al., 2019</xref>).</p>
      <p>Viral loads reached 10<sup>5</sup> copies per mL, with <inline-formula><mml:math><mml:mi>n</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>12</mml:mn></mml:math></inline-formula> animals.<fig id="F1"><label>Fig. 1</label><caption><title>Trapping sites.</title><p>Sites in the valley<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="R4">4</xref>.</p></caption></fig></p>
    </sec>
    <sec id="s2" sec-type="methods">
      <title>Methods</title>
      <p>Traps were set every night.</p>
      <sec id="s2-1">
        <title>Sequencing</title>
        <p>Samples were sequenced in 2020.</p>
      </sec>
      <p>All analyses used R.</p>
    </sec>
    <sec id="s3">
      <title>Discussion</title>
      <p>Density predicts spillover.</p>
      <boxed-text id="B1">
        <label>Box 1</label>
        <caption><title>Glossary of terms</title></caption>
        <p>Spillover is transmission from one host species to another.</p>
